#!perl
use v5.36;
use Test::Mutation::CLI;

exit Test::Mutation::CLI::main(@ARGV);

__END__

=head1 NAME

mutation - measure how many mutants the tests of a Perl project detect

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    mutation baseline
    mutation run
    mutation list
    mutation help run

Inspect the available mutators and configuration defaults when choosing how to measure a project.

    mutation mutators
    mutation settings

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Run C<mutation> in a distribution or pass its directory with C<-C>.
The terminal report names a change to inspect with C<mutation show> and the test that detected it when available.
C<mutation exec> runs the requested command in a private project copy, including when the measurement configuration selects C<--in-place>.
L<Test::Mutation> describes the Perl API.

=head1 RECHECK A MISSED CHANGE

Suppose a test expects C<sum(4, 0)> to return 4.
Replacing addition with subtraction gives the same answer.
Run C<mutation run> and inspect the patch and the tests under the surviving change.
Add a test that expects C<sum(4, 1)> to return 5, then run the saved gaps again.

    mutation run --undetected

The command rechecks surviving and uncovered mutants from the last completed run or merge.
It confirms a detection or survival with a second attempt.
When no gaps remain, the command reports zero mutants without starting tests.
Use C<mutation show> or C<mutation list> to read a dated result from another measurement.
Each new report scores its own selection.

Within the project, the report directory stores output digests in C<.test-mutation-outputs> so copied or edited reports count as test inputs.
With C<--in-place>, write shard reports outside the project using C<--report-dir> so all shards measure the same inputs.

=head1 PROGRESS

The active phase and the elapsed time go to standard error.
A terminal uses color by default.
C<--color> enables color for redirected output, and a nonempty C<NO_COLOR> disables it.
Use the following setting to keep progress and reports plain.

    color = false

=head1 GITHUB ACTIONS

C<--github> writes workflow annotations to standard error.
When C<GITHUB_ACTIONS> is C<true>, annotations are enabled by default.
The C<GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY> file receives the measured scope, score, gate, gap count, examples and paths to the detailed reports.
Use the following setting to request GitHub output explicitly.

    github = true

=head1 TEST EXECUTION

The project can supply library directories through C<.proverc>, C<--lib>, or the configuration file.

    lib = ["lib", "t/lib"]

The driver adds the selected directories to C<PERL5LIB>.
With taint checking, the interpreter receives the directories, inherited C<PERL5OPT> switches, and the shim on its command line.
Selecting a build library sets C<PERL_DL_NONLAZY> for the test process.
C<HARNESS_RULESFILE> selects the scheduling rules used by C<prove> when tests need exclusive access.
C<TEST_MUTATION_ROOT> identifies the current test copy.
C<TEST_MUTATION_GENERATION> selects its shim when a test starts another measurement.

=head1 COMMANDS

=for generated COMMANDS

=over 4

=item C<mutation [run] [PATH[:LINES]...] [options]>

Measure how many mutants the tests detect (the default).

=item C<mutation baseline [options]>

Run the unmodified tests and report failures.

=item C<mutation list [PATH[:LINES]...] [options]>

Print the mutants without running a test.

=item C<mutation show ID [options]>

Print one mutant as a unified diff.

=item C<mutation exec ID [options] -- COMMAND [ARGUMENT...]>

Run a command with one mutant active.

=item C<mutation merge FILE... [options]>

Report one run from the mutation.json of every part of --shard.

=item C<mutation mutators [options]>

Print the name, the profile, and the description of every mutator.

=item C<mutation settings>

Print the available settings and their defaults.

=item C<mutation help [COMMAND]>

Show the usage and options of a command.

=item C<mutation --help>

Print this text.

=item C<mutation --version>

Print the version.

=back

=for end generated COMMANDS

=head1 SETTINGS

=for generated SETTINGS

=over 4

=item C<--root, -C>

Run as if mutation had started in DIR.
The Perl argument is C<root>.
The default is C<.>.

=item C<--config>

Configuration file to read in place of mutation.toml.
The Perl argument is C<config>.
The default is mutation.toml when it exists.

=item C<--jobs, -j>

Test processes to run side by side.
The configuration key is C<jobs>.
The Perl argument is C<jobs>.
The default is one less than the processors, and at least one.

=item C<--github, --no-github>

Write GitHub Actions annotations and a job summary.
The configuration key is C<github>.
The Perl argument is C<github>.
The default is on inside GitHub Actions.

=item C<--color, --no-color>

Color the progress and the terminal report.
The configuration key is C<color>.
The Perl argument is C<color>.
The default is on for a terminal without NO_COLOR.

=item C<--min-score>

Fail when the score is below N percent.
The configuration key is C<min-score>.
The Perl argument is C<min_score>.
The default is none.

=item C<--format>

Report to write: terminal, json, or html; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<format>.
The Perl argument is C<format>.
The default is terminal json html.

=item C<--report-dir>

Directory for the report files.
The configuration key is C<report-dir>.
The Perl argument is C<report_dir>.
The default is mutation-report.

=item C<--threshold-high>

Score from which a report marks the result as good.
The configuration key is C<threshold-high>.
The Perl argument is C<threshold_high>.
The default is 80.

=item C<--threshold-low>

Score below which a report marks the result as poor.
The configuration key is C<threshold-low>.
The Perl argument is C<threshold_low>.
The default is 60.

=item C<--perl>

Interpreter that runs the tests.
The configuration key is C<perl>.
The Perl argument is C<perl>.
The default is the running interpreter.

=item C<--lib>

Directory to search for Perl modules.
The configuration key is C<lib>.
The Perl argument is C<lib>.
The default is lib, or blib for a distribution that builds.

=item C<--switch>

Switch of the interpreter of the tests; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<switch>.
The Perl argument is C<switch>.
The default is none.

=item C<--source>

Files to mutate; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<source>.
The Perl argument is C<source>.
The default is lib/**/*.pm and the Perl scripts of bin and script.

=item C<--source-exclude>

Files to leave unmutated; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<source-exclude>.
The Perl argument is C<source_exclude>.
The default is none.

=item C<--test>

Test files; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<test>.
The Perl argument is C<test>.
The default is t/**/*.t.

=item C<--test-exclude>

Test files to leave out; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<test-exclude>.
The Perl argument is C<test_exclude>.
The default is none.

=item C<--profile>

Mutator profile: default or extended.
The configuration key is C<profile>.
The Perl argument is C<profile>.
The default is default.

=item C<--enable>

Add a mutator; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<enable>.
The Perl argument is C<enable>.
The default is none.

=item C<--disable>

Remove a mutator; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<disable>.
The Perl argument is C<disable>.
The default is none.

=item C<--equivalence, --no-equivalence>

Leave out the mutants that compile to the code of the original.
The configuration key is C<equivalence>.
The Perl argument is C<equivalence>.
The default is on.

=item C<--suppress-require-names, --no-suppress-require-names>

Require each suppression comment to name its mutators.
The configuration key is C<suppress-require-names>.
The Perl argument is C<suppress_require_names>.
The default is off.

=item C<--suppress-require-reason, --no-suppress-require-reason>

Require each suppression comment to give a reason.
The configuration key is C<suppress-require-reason>.
The Perl argument is C<suppress_require_reason>.
The default is off.

=item C<--coverage>

Select tests by line or by file.
The configuration key is C<coverage>.
The Perl argument is C<coverage>.
The default is line.

=item C<--timeout-factor>

Time limit as a multiple of the unmodified duration.
The configuration key is C<timeout-factor>.
The Perl argument is C<timeout_factor>.
The default is 4.

=item C<--timeout-floor>

Smallest time limit in seconds.
The configuration key is C<timeout-floor>.
The Perl argument is C<timeout_floor>.
The default is 10.

=item C<--baseline-timeout>

Time limit in seconds for a test of the unmodified run.
The configuration key is C<baseline-timeout>.
The Perl argument is C<baseline_timeout>.
The default is 600.

=item C<--in-place, --no-in-place>

Run the tests in the project itself, not in copies of it.
The configuration key is C<in-place>.
The Perl argument is C<in_place>.
The default is off.

=item C<--link>

Path that a copy holds as a link to the project; repeatable.
The configuration key is C<link>.
The Perl argument is C<link>.
The default is local.

=item C<--keep-work, --no-keep-work>

Keep the work directory after the run.
The configuration key is C<keep-work>.
The Perl argument is C<keep_work>.
The default is off.

=item C<--mutant>

Rerun the named mutant and confirm a kill or survival with a second attempt (repeatable).
The configuration key is C<mutant>.
The Perl argument is C<mutant>.
The default is none.

=item C<--undetected, --no-undetected>

Recheck surviving and uncovered mutants from the last completed measurement.
The configuration key is C<undetected>.
The Perl argument is C<undetected>.
The default is off.

=item C<--since>

Cover the lines changed since a Git revision.
The configuration key is C<since>.
The Perl argument is C<since>.
The default is none.

=item C<--shard>

Cover part K of N equal parts of the mutants.
The configuration key is C<shard>.
The Perl argument is C<shard>.
The default is none.

=item C<--cache, --no-cache>

Reuse the results of an earlier run where the inputs are unchanged.
The configuration key is C<cache>.
The Perl argument is C<cache>.
The default is on.

=item C<--cache-dir>

Directory for cached measurement results.
The configuration key is C<cache-dir>.
The Perl argument is C<cache_dir>.
The default is .mutation.

=back

=for end generated SETTINGS

=head1 STATUSES

=for generated STATUSES

=over 4

=item C<Killed>

A test that loaded the mutant failed.
The report schema uses C<Killed>.

=item C<Survived>

Every test that loaded the mutant passed.
The report schema uses C<Survived>.

=item C<NoCoverage>

No test loaded the mutant.
The report schema uses C<NoCoverage>.

=item C<Timeout>

A test that loaded the mutant exceeded its time limit or printed past its output limit, and no test failed.
The report schema uses C<Ignored>.

=item C<CompileError>

The mutated file fails to compile.
The report schema uses C<CompileError>.

=item C<NoVerdict>

The run could not decide, for the cause that the reason names.
The report schema uses C<RuntimeError>.

=item C<Ignored>

A comment suppressed the mutant, or it compiles to code that the run measured already.
The report schema uses C<Ignored>.

=item C<Pending>

The run ended before the mutant.
The report schema uses C<Pending>.

=back

=for end generated STATUSES

=head1 EXIT STATUS

=for generated EXIT STATUS

=over 4

=item C<status 0>

The command did its work.

=item C<status 1>

The score is below the one that --min-score asks for.

=item C<status 2>

The command refuses its arguments, its configuration, or a file that they name.

=item C<status 3>

A test fails without a mutant.

=item C<status 4>

The run states no score.

=item C<status 5>

A file, Git, or a process kept the command from its work.

=item C<status 6>

An error inside mutation, which is a defect of the tool.

=item C<status 125>

Mutation exec failed apart from the command that it runs.

=item C<status 126>

The command of mutation exec exists, and the system refuses to run it.

=item C<status 127>

Mutation exec finds no command of the given name.

=item C<status 129>

The signal HUP ended the run.

=item C<status 130>

The signal INT ended the run.

=item C<status 131>

The signal QUIT ended the run.

=item C<status 141>

The signal PIPE ended the run.

=item C<status 143>

The signal TERM ended the run.

=back

=for end generated EXIT STATUS

=head1 ENVIRONMENT

=for generated ENVIRONMENT

=over 4

=item C<PATH>

Looks up the selected Perl interpreter and programs invoked by the tests.

=item C<PERL5LIB>

Adds library directories inherited by Perl processes.

=item C<PERL5OPT>

Supplies inherited interpreter switches before the measurement shim loads.

=item C<PERL_DL_NONLAZY>

Requests eager XS symbol resolution for a build-library test.

=item C<HARNESS_ACTIVE>

Identifies a child test started by the harness.

=item C<HARNESS_VERSION>

Gives child tests the installed TAP harness version.

=item C<HARNESS_RULESFILE>

Selects the TAP harness scheduling rules for parallel and sequential tests.

=item C<TMPDIR>

Selects the temporary parent and points each running test at its private temporary directory.

=item C<TMP>

Can select the temporary parent on platforms where File::Spec reads TMP.

=item C<TEMP>

Can select the temporary parent on platforms where File::Spec reads TEMP.

=item C<PWD>

Names the working directory of the child test.

=item C<NO_COLOR>

Disables color when its value is nonempty.

=item C<GITHUB_ACTIONS>

Enables GitHub annotations by default when its value is true.

=item C<GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY>

Receives the Markdown report for the current GitHub Actions step.

=item C<TEST_MUTATION_ROOT>

Identifies the measured project inside a running test.

=item C<TEST_MUTATION_GENERATION>

Selects the shim generation for a nested measurement.

=item C<TEST_MUTATION_COMPILER_INPUTS>

The driver sets the path where a standalone Perl compiler records additional library inputs.

=item C<TEST_MUTATION_COMPILER_READS>

Names the file operands that the target Perl resolves during compilation.

=item C<TEST_MUTATION_SPEC>

Names the measurement instructions for the unnumbered shim.

=item C<TEST_MUTATION_RECORD>

Names the process record directory for the unnumbered shim.

=item C<TEST_MUTATION_SPEC_Gn>

Names the measurement instructions for shim generation n.

=item C<TEST_MUTATION_RECORD_Gn>

Names the process record directory for shim generation n.

=item C<PERL_HASH_SEED>

The compiler comparison uses a fixed seed for reproducible deparsing.

=item C<PERL_PERTURB_KEYS>

The compiler comparison uses a fixed hash traversal mode.

=item C<OLDPWD>

The previous shell working directory is excluded from the cache key.

=item C<SHLVL>

The shell nesting level is excluded from the cache key.

=item C<_>

The previous shell command is excluded from the cache key.

=item C<GITHUB_RUN_ID>

The cache key excludes the value assigned by the runner.

=item C<GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER>

The cache key excludes the value assigned by the runner.

=item C<GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT>

The cache key excludes the value assigned by the runner.

=item C<RUNNER_TEMP>

The cache key excludes the value assigned by the runner.

=item C<RUNNER_TRACKING_ID>

The cache key excludes the value assigned by the runner.

=item C<CI_JOB_ID>

The cache key excludes the value assigned by the runner.

=item C<CI_PIPELINE_ID>

The cache key excludes the value assigned by the runner.

=back

=for end generated ENVIRONMENT

=head1 DIAGNOSTICS

=for generated DIAGNOSTICS

=over 4

=item C<build-recipe-missing>

C<XS sources need Makefile.PL or Build.PL; build the distribution separately and use -C to select its build directory>
An XS distribution needs a configuration script in its source directory.

=item C<build-product-stale>

C<{path} needs a rebuild; run {command} before in-place measurement>
An in-place build product predates its source.

=item C<build-copy-not-updated>

C<{path} in the private build did not update after changing {source}>
The generated product kept its original contents after the mutant was installed.

=item C<build-command-failed>

C<{command} failed: {reason}>
A command that builds the private distribution failed.

=item C<command-unknown>

C<unknown command {command}, did you mean {nearest}?>
The first word or the command named in help has no matching command.

=item C<in-place-observation>

C<--in-place uses the module hook, which can miss absolute paths and child processes>
A project run reports the observation limits of its module hook.

=item C<path-selector-invalid>

C<invalid path selection {path}: {reason}>
A path operand must name a project source or directory and use positive, ascending line ranges.

=item C<argument-unexpected>

C<unexpected argument {argument}>
The command line holds an operand, and the command takes none.

=item C<show-without-identifier>

C<show needs a mutant identifier>
The command line of show names no mutant.

=item C<exec-without-command>

C<exec needs a mutant identifier and a command>
The command line of exec names no mutant or no command to run.

=item C<merge-without-report>

C<merge needs the report of every part>
The command line of merge names no report file.

=item C<directory-closed>

C<cannot enter {directory}: {reason}>
The directory that --root names is absent, is no directory, or refuses the user.

=item C<measurement-history-missing>

C<no completed measurement is available for --undetected. Run mutation run first>
The command needs a completed scope before it can recheck undetected changes.

=item C<mutant-history-stale>

C<mutant {identifier} is no longer present. Run mutation run {path} to measure the file again>
The source no longer contains a mutant from the last completed measurement.

=item C<mutant-unknown>

C<no mutant has the identifier {identifier}>
No mutant of the project has an identifier that starts with the given text.

=item C<mutant-ambiguous>

C<more than one mutant has the identifier {identifier}>
Several mutants have an identifier that starts with the given text.

=item C<mutator-unknown>

C<unknown mutator: {names}>
A setting names a mutator that the tool lacks.

=item C<prove-exec-unsupported>

C<.proverc: --exec replaces the Perl interpreter needed to measure mutants>
The project requests an external test executor in its prove configuration.

=item C<glob-unmatched>

C<{setting} pattern "{pattern}" matches no project file>
An explicitly supplied include or exclude pattern must match a file in the project.

=item C<interpreter-invalid>

C<cannot use "{program}" as the Perl interpreter>
The selected program must report Perl 5.10 or later to load the test shim.

=item C<revision-unknown>

C<no commit matches revision "{revision}">
The revision passed to --since must identify a commit in the project repository.

=item C<source-changed>

C<{path} changed after analysis>
The bytes of the copied source differ from the bytes used to generate its mutants.

=item C<measurement-inputs-changed>

C<measurement inputs changed while preparing the copy ({path}), so rerun the measurement>
The prepared inputs must match the project data and test bytes recorded during analysis.

=item C<option-unknown>

C<{option} names no option; the nearest is {nearest}>
The command line holds an option that the command lacks, and the message names the option nearest to it.

=item C<option-elsewhere>

C<{option} is no option of {command}>
The command line holds an option of another command.

=item C<option-without-value>

C<{option} needs a value>
An option that takes a value stands at the end of the command line or before an option.

=item C<option-with-value>

C<{option} takes no value>
An option that is on or off has a value after an equals sign.

=item C<setting-unknown>

C<{setting} names no setting; the nearest is {nearest}>
The configuration file or a call of the library names a setting that the tool lacks.

=item C<setting-invalid>

C<{setting} takes {expects}, not {value}>
A setting has a value outside its type, which the message states as the user wrote it.

=item C<setting-outside-file>

C<{setting} names the place of the file, so the file cannot hold it>
The configuration file holds the directory of the project or the name of a configuration file.

=item C<config-section>

C<{file}: [{section}] is a section, and the file holds none>
The configuration file holds a table, and its settings stand at its top level.

=item C<settings-out-of-order>

C<{first} lies above {second}>
The low threshold of the reports lies above the high one.

=item C<settings-exclusive>

C<{first} and {second} exclude each other>
Two settings that the user set cannot hold in one run.

=item C<working-directory-gone>

C<the working directory is gone, so --root must name the project>
The process stands in a directory that no longer exists, and the command line names no project.

=item C<input-unreadable>

C<cannot read {path}: {reason}>
A file that the user names, such as the configuration file or a report, cannot be read.

=item C<input-malformed>

C<{path}: {reason}>
A file that the user names holds a text that the tool cannot use, for the reason that follows its name.

=item C<merge-empty>

C<merge needs at least one report>
A merge got no report.

=item C<merge-repeated-mutant>

C<mutant {id} appears in more than one part>
Two reports of a merge hold the same mutant.

=item C<merge-part-size>

C<{name} holds {held} mutants, and part {part} of {parts} has {expected}>
A report of a merge holds another number of mutants than its part of the run has.

=item C<merge-whole-run>

C<{name} is the report of a whole run, not of a part>
A report of a merge comes from a run without --shard.

=item C<merge-without-inventory>

C<{name} names no inventory>
A report of a merge lacks the count and the digest of the mutants of its run.

=item C<merge-interrupted>

C<{name} was interrupted by SIG{signal}, rerun that shard before merging>
A shard received a signal before measurement finished.

=item C<merge-failed-baseline>

C<{name} has failing tests in its unmodified run, so its part measured nothing>
A report of a merge comes from a part whose tests fail without a mutant.

=item C<merge-other-split>

C<{first} holds part {first_part} of {first_parts}, and {name} holds part {part} of {parts}>
Two reports of a merge come from runs that split the mutants into different numbers of parts.

=item C<merge-other-mutants>

C<{name} covers other mutants than {first}>
Two reports of a merge come from runs over different mutants.

=item C<merge-repeated-part>

C<{first} and {name} both hold part {part} of {parts}>
Two reports of a merge hold the same part of the run.

=item C<merge-missing-part>

C<part {part} of {parts} is missing>
The reports of a merge lack one part of the run.

=item C<merge-missing-parts>

C<parts {list} of {parts} are missing>
The reports of a merge lack several parts of the run.

=item C<report-not-json>

C<the text is no JSON document>
A report file holds a text that is no JSON object.

=item C<report-without-facts>

C<the report holds no facts of a mutation run>
A report file is a JSON document that this tool did not write.

=item C<report-invalid-value>

C<the report is malformed: no valid value at {place}>
A value of a report file has another type than the report format gives it.

=item C<report-malformed>

C<the report is malformed: {reason}>
A report file holds facts that form no result of a run.

=item C<report-location-outside>

C<the report is malformed: a location lies outside the source of {path}>
A mutant of a report file starts or ends outside the text of its source file.

=item C<report-location-reversed>

C<the report is malformed: a location ends before its start in {path}>
A mutant of a report file ends before it starts.

=item C<suppressions-invalid>

C<invalid suppression comments: {list}>
Comments of the analyzed files break the grammar of the suppression comments or suppress no mutant.

=item C<suppression-malformed>

C<{path}:{line}: "{comment}" differs from the grammar>
A comment resembles a suppression comment and has another form than the grammar.

=item C<suppression-mutator-unknown>

C<{path}:{line}: unknown mutator {names}>
A suppression comment names a mutator that the tool lacks.

=item C<suppression-end-unopened>

C<{path}:{line}: ## use mutate ends no open block>
A comment ends a block of suppressed lines that no comment opened.

=item C<suppression-block-nested>

C<{path}:{line}: ## no mutate opens a block inside the block of line {open}>
A comment opens a block of suppressed lines while an earlier block is still open.

=item C<suppression-block-unclosed>

C<{path}:{line}: ## no mutate opens a block that no ## use mutate ends>
A block of suppressed lines is still open at the end of its file.

=item C<suppression-sub-missing>

C<{path}:{line}: ## no mutate sub stands before no named subroutine or method>
The statement after a comment that suppresses a subroutine is no named subroutine or method.

=item C<suppression-unused>

C<{path}:{line}: "{comment}" suppresses no mutant>
A suppression comment covers no mutant that a mutator of the tool makes.

=item C<suppression-names-missing>

C<{path}:{line}: "{comment}" names no mutator, and suppress-require-names asks for one>
A suppression comment names no mutator, and the setting suppress-require-names asks for a name.

=item C<suppression-reason-missing>

C<{path}:{line}: "{comment}" gives no reason, and suppress-require-reason asks for one>
A suppression comment gives no reason, and the setting suppress-require-reason asks for one.

=item C<file-unreadable>

C<cannot read {path}: {reason}>
A file or a directory of the project or of the run cannot be read.

=item C<file-unwritable>

C<cannot write {path}: {reason}>
A file of the run or of a report cannot be written.

=item C<file-unclosable>

C<cannot close {path}: {reason}>
A file reports an error when it closes, so its content may be incomplete.

=item C<file-unrenamable>

C<cannot rename {path}: {reason}>
A file that the tool wrote cannot take its final name.

=item C<file-unremovable>

C<cannot remove {path}: {reason}>
A file or a directory of a copy cannot be removed.

=item C<file-unlinkable>

C<cannot link {path}: {reason}>
A copy of the project cannot hold a link.

=item C<file-uncopyable>

C<cannot copy {source} to {target}: {reason}>
A file of the project cannot be copied.

=item C<file-time-unsettable>

C<cannot set the time of {path}: {reason}>
A copied file cannot take the time of its original.

=item C<file-mode-unsettable>

C<cannot set the mode of {path}: {reason}>
A copied file or directory cannot take the mode of its original.

=item C<directory-missing>

C<cannot read {path}: no such directory>
The directory to copy is absent.

=item C<directory-uncreatable>

C<cannot create {path}: {reason}>
A directory of the run, of a copy, or of a report cannot be created.

=item C<directory-unopenable>

C<cannot open {path}: {reason}>
A directory of a copy refuses the rights that the tool needs to change it.

=item C<project-uncopyable>

C<cannot copy the project: {reason}; --in-place runs the tests in the project itself>
The copy of the project that the tests run in cannot be made.

=item C<git-unstartable>

C<cannot start git: {reason}>
The git program cannot be started.

=item C<git-failed>

C<git {command} failed: {reason}>
A git command failed, with the first line of its error.

=item C<git-silent>

C<git {command} failed>
A git command failed and left no error to read.

=item C<process-unstartable>

C<cannot start a process: {reason}>
The system refuses a new process for a test.

=item C<pipe-unopenable>

C<cannot open a pipe: {reason}>
The system refuses the pipe that carries the output of a test.

=item C<pipe-unclosable>

C<cannot close a pipe: {reason}>
The pipe that carries the output of a test reports an error when it closes.

=item C<test-directory-closed>

C<cannot run the test in {directory}: {reason}>
The process of a test cannot enter the directory that the test runs in.

=item C<test-streams-unset>

C<cannot set the streams of the test: {reason}>
The process of a test cannot open its standard streams.

=item C<command-unstartable>

C<cannot start {command}: {reason}>
The interpreter of the tests cannot be started.

=item C<command-not-runnable>

C<cannot run {command}: {reason}>
The command of mutation exec exists, and the system refuses to run it.

=item C<command-not-found>

C<cannot find {command}: {reason}>
No program of the name that mutation exec runs exists at its path or on the search path.

=item C<spec-unreadable>

C<cannot read spec {path}: {reason}>
A test process cannot read the file that names the mutants and the lines of its run.

=item C<spec-unclosable>

C<cannot close spec {path}: {reason}>
A test process reports an error when it closes the file that names the mutants and the lines of its run.

=item C<analysis-left-out-place>

C<{path}: the analysis left out 1 place, which has no mutant>
The analysis of a file left one place out, so the code there has no mutant.

=item C<analysis-left-out-places>

C<{path}: the analysis left out {count} places, which have no mutant>
The analysis of a file left several places out, so the code there has no mutant.

=item C<work-directory-stays>

C<the work directory {path} stays, because a file in it resists removal>
A test left a file in the work directory that the run cannot remove.

=item C<cache-verdict-changed>

C<the cached verdict for {id} differs from a fresh measurement, so every selected mutant was measured again>
The stored verdicts were discarded after a verification sample disagreed.

=item C<tests-unmatched>

C<no test files match>
The project has mutants and no test file to run.

=item C<tests-conflict>

C<the tests fail when they run side by side, so the mutants run one test at a time>
The tests pass one at a time and fail side by side, so the run gives up the parallel jobs.

=item C<conflicting-test>

C<{test} passed alone after an overlapping failure, so later invocations run one at a time>
The isolated repeat passed after simultaneous invocations of the same test interfered.

=item C<loaded-around-hook>

C<{test} loads {path} around the hook and cannot detect its mutants>
A test reads a source file in a way that passes by the module that places the mutants.

=item C<project-changed>

C<the project changed while the mutants ran: {changes}>
A mutant test changed the project in place and the runner restored the affected paths.

=item C<decompiled-code-unstable>

C<{path}: Perl decompiles the file to no stable text, so the run compares no compiled code of the file>
The original file compiled without deparsed output, so the run skips the equivalence check for its mutants.

=item C<syntax-check-failed>

C<{path} does not pass a standalone syntax check, so its load failures count as compile errors>
A source file fails a syntax check of its own, so a mutant that fails to load counts as a compile error.

=item C<invalid-mutant>

C<invalid mutant {id} in {path}>
Perl rejected a replacement or a function that the replacement introduced.

=item C<internal-error>

C<internal error: {text}>
An error that the tool did not foresee ended the command.

=item C<message-slots>

C<the message {id} takes the slots {expected}, and the call gives {given}>
The code asks for a message with other values than its text holds.

=item C<class-argument-unknown>

C<the class {class} takes no argument {names}>
The code builds a value with an argument that names none of its attributes.

=item C<shim-unprepared>

C<cannot prepare the shim for generation {generation}>
The text of the shim lacks the lines that a run rewrites.

=item C<shim-missing>

C<cannot find {file}>
The module search path holds no file of the shim.

=item C<verdict-without-syntax-check>

C<a load failure needs a syntax check>
The verdict of a mutant got a failed load without the result of its syntax check.

=item C<below-gate>

C<the score {score}% is below the gate of {gate}%>
The run measured a score below the one that --min-score asks for.

=item C<baseline-failed>

C<tests that fail without a mutant: {failed}>
Tests fail in the unmodified run, so the run measured no mutant.

=item C<inconclusive>

C<the run states no score>
The run ended without a score that the user can rely on.

=item C<interrupted>

C<interrupted by SIG{signal}>
A signal ended the command before its work was done.

=back

=for end generated DIAGNOSTICS

=head1 MUTATORS

Use C<--enable early-exit> to test guarded transfers of control with the default profile.
C<--enable @removal> selects it with the other removal mutators.

=for generated MUTATORS

=over 4

=item C<arithmetic>

Replaces an arithmetic operator with its counterpart.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<comparison-boundary>

Moves the boundary of a numeric or string comparison.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<comparison-negation>

Negates a numeric or string comparison.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<logical>

Replaces a logical operator with its counterpart.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<bitwise>

Replaces a bitwise operator with its counterpart or removes a complement.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<increment>

Swaps increment and decrement.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<assignment>

Replaces a compound assignment with its counterpart.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<loop-control>

Swaps last and next, and turns redo into next.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<call-swap>

Replaces a function with its counterpart.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<boolean-literal>

Swaps true and false.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<shift-pop>

Swaps shift and pop.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<list-order>

Reverses a sort or removes an explicit reverse.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<call-removal>

Removes abs or int while keeping its argument.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<three-way>

Swaps the operands of a three-way comparison.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<early-exit>

Removes a guarded transfer of control.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<filter-negation>

Negates the predicate of grep or first.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<regex-class>

Complements a character class in a pattern.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<literal-collection>

Empties an array, hash, or word-list literal.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<unary-minus>

Removes a unary minus or the sign of a number.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<condition-negation>

Negates a condition or swaps the branches of a conditional.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<predicate>

Negates a test of definedness, existence, context, or a file.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<negation>

Removes a logical negation.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<match>

Negates a pattern match.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<return-value>

Removes a return value or flips a literal 0 or 1.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<statement-removal>

Removes an expression statement.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<sub-body>

Removes the body of a subroutine.
The C<default> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<close-removal>

Removes a close or closedir statement.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<number-literal>

Advances a numeric literal in its original base.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<string-literal>

Empties a string literal.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<condition-fixed>

Replaces a condition with a constant.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<range-boundary>

Moves the endpoint of a range or flip-flop.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<regex-anchor>

Removes an anchor from a pattern.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<regex-quantifier>

Changes a quantifier in a pattern.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=item C<regex-flag>

Removes a matching or substitution flag.
The C<extended> profile includes the mutator.

=back

=for end generated MUTATORS

